<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qian Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/929">929</a>–<a href="/years/988">988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qian Chu was the last king of Wuyue, the coastal kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, reigning from 947 to 978. He helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> conquer Southern Tang and in 978 handed his entire kingdom to Song without a fight, so that Wuyue's lands were spared war. He was honoured with titles at the Song court for the rest of his life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qian-chu.jpg" alt="Qian Chu" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 松照庵 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Qian_Hongchu_King_Zhongyi_of_Wuyue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Qian Chu, courtesy name Wende, was born on 29 September 929 and was known as Qian Hongchu until 960. He came to power after his brother Qian Zong was deposed in a coup. Wuyue was then at its largest, ruling 13 prefectures in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. Like its earlier kings he never took the title of emperor and nominally submitted to the dominant northern regime, which in return respected Wuyue's autonomy and gave its kings high honours such as Commander of All Horses and Soldiers Under Heaven.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> unified the north, Qian Chu is said to have followed the instruction of his ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qian-liu">Qian Liu</a> to submit as soon as the true lord appeared. He submitted to Song in 960 and dropped the character hong from his name, as it was taboo under the new dynasty. On the orders of the Song court he took part in the annexation of the other southern kingdoms, and in 977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-song">Emperor Taizong of Song</a> gave him the nominal titles of Chancellor and Chief Secretary.</p><p>His surrender of 978 may have been made under veiled threat, but it spared the region the wars that struck other states and preserved the infrastructure and wealth built up under Wuyue. This contributed in no small part to the Yangtze Delta becoming the economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>To allay suspicion he lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> capital, Bianjing, with 3,000 members of his household, while his sons and many of the Wuyue elite received imperial posts. He kept a series of nominal royal titles, King of Huaihai, then of Hannan in 984 and of Nanyang in 987, before being made Prince of Deng in 988. He was often invited to banquets and ball games at the palace.</p><p>On his 60th birthday, by the Chinese count, the emperor sent him wine; after drinking it he fell violently ill and died that night, on 7 October 988. He received a state funeral, was posthumously named King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and was buried near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. A devout Buddhist and a writer of poetry, he ordered the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leifeng-pagoda">Leifeng Pagoda</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qian Chu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qian Chu</b>, the last king, helped the Song Dynasty conquer Southern Tang and in 978 surrendered his whole kingdom to Song without bloodshed.</blockquote><a href="/state/wuyue">Wuyue</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hang Prefecture, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Deng Prefecture, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.27,120.17&amp;year=967" aria-label="Hang Prefecture on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7668 1059 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qian Chu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8118 1329h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8118 1329h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.27,120.17&amp;year=967">What was here in 967 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
